00:00:20.086 --> 00:00:21.788 line:92%
I like that you have the title

00:00:21.788 --> 00:00:23.623 line:92%
of the book over your shoulder

00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:25.425 align:start size:94% position:6%
That is very neat.
-Oh, did I?

00:00:25.425 --> 00:00:29.496
Oh, I didn't even -- I didn'
even know that I did that.

00:00:29.496 --> 00:00:31.331 align:start size:94% position:6%
I didn't even --
-You're a natural.

00:00:31.331 --> 00:00:33.266 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're a natural
at self-promotion.

00:00:33.266 --> 00:00:34.034 size:75% position:38% line:92%
I really tip my cap.

00:00:34.034 --> 00:00:37.404 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I just put the painting
up there on the wall.

00:00:37.404 --> 00:00:38.772 align:start size:94% position:6%
By the way,
do you know what this is?

00:00:38.772 --> 00:00:44.677 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is a hat that Tug McGra
gave to Henry Winkler.

00:00:44.677 --> 00:00:45.111 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Really?

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:47.914 size:84% position:42%
And then you broke into
Henry Winkler's house?

00:00:47.914 --> 00:00:50.950 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-Henry Winkler, you know,

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:54.354
discovered that I was a Phillies
fan and sent it to me.

00:00:54.354 --> 00:00:57.257 size:84% position:42%
-That's really nice.
Did Tug McGraw sign it?

00:00:57.257 --> 00:00:58.925 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-It says 45 on it,

00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:00.527 align:start size:94% position:6%
which I think was his number
but he did not sign it.

00:01:00.527 --> 00:01:02.162 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So anybody could've
written that.

00:01:02.162 --> 00:01:04.497 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It has sweat stains though,
so I assume those are

00:01:04.497 --> 00:01:06.900 align:start size:94% position:6%
actually Tug McGraw's
sweat stains.

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:07.634 size:88% position:44% line:92%
-This is the only thing.

00:01:07.634 --> 00:01:11.871 size:84% position:42%
So, Henry Winkler heard
I was a Red Sox fan,

00:01:11.871 --> 00:01:14.974 align:start size:94% position:6%
and he sent me a hat
and he said it was Babe Ruth's

00:01:14.974 --> 00:01:16.309 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:16.309 --> 00:01:17.177 line:92%
I think what I'm saying here i

00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:20.313 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think we should not
necessarily trust Henry Winkle

00:01:20.313 --> 00:01:22.749 align:start size:94% position:6%
when he sends us
an old, sweaty hat.

00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:23.983 size:81% position:41% line:92%
-But here's the thing.

00:01:23.983 --> 00:01:26.653 size:81% position:41%
I did do some research
because you know me.

00:01:26.653 --> 00:01:27.587 align:start size:94% position:6%
I like to research.
-Yeah.

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:32.692 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And the cast of "Happy Days
actually did do, like,

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:36.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
promotional baseball games
in the late '70s,

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:40.233 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they did actually go t
Philadelphia, I think, in '79.

00:01:40.233 --> 00:01:42.602 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, I mean, it checks out
is all I'm saying.

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:46.039 align:start size:94% position:6%
It checks out.
-It lightly checks out.

00:01:46.039 --> 00:01:47.040 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughs ]

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.476 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I couldn't find any evidenc
that Tug McGraw

00:01:49.476 --> 00:01:51.911
gave Henry Winkler the hat
but I also couldn't find

00:01:51.911 --> 00:01:56.549 align:start size:94% position:6%
any reason why he would
concoct the story

00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:59.252 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then send me
a sweaty ball cap.

00:01:59.252 --> 00:01:59.853 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-You know what?

00:01:59.853 --> 00:02:04.157
I think the takeaway here should
be a reminder to all our viewers

00:02:04.157 --> 00:02:08.128 align:start size:94% position:6%
that Henry Winkler is
possibly the nicest person

00:02:08.128 --> 00:02:10.130 align:start size:94% position:6%
not only in show business
but any business.

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:11.731 align:start size:94% position:6%
-In the world.
-In the world.

00:02:11.731 --> 00:02:15.835 align:start size:94% position:6%
-100%.
The nicest guy I've ever met

00:02:15.835 --> 00:02:19.606 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I can't even understand.
Like, he's the Fonz.

00:02:19.606 --> 00:02:22.041 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because the Fonz
wasn't particularly nice.

00:02:22.041 --> 00:02:22.976 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-No.

00:02:22.976 --> 00:02:24.511 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, he was helpful,
you know,

00:02:24.511 --> 00:02:27.514 align:start size:94% position:6%
whenever the jukebox
wasn't working.

00:02:27.514 --> 00:02:30.483 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
He would give it a bang.

00:02:30.483 --> 00:02:32.452 line:92%
And that was good for the gang

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:35.155
-Not the only thing he gav
a bang to, if you know what I --

00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:36.356 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-Okay, you know what?

00:02:36.356 --> 00:02:39.692
I'm pretty sure that didn'
actually happen on the show.

00:02:39.692 --> 00:02:41.494 align:start size:94% position:6%
So, this is --
I want to talk about this

00:02:41.494 --> 00:02:44.664 align:start size:94% position:6%
because you wrote the first of
your books, a best seller.

00:02:44.664 --> 00:02:45.532 align:start size:94% position:6%
Congratulations.
-Thank you.

00:02:45.532 --> 00:02:47.233 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And did you write that
with any sense

00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:49.936 align:start size:94% position:6%
that you were gonna
write another one?

00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:50.670 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-No.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:53.540 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, I thought,
"I hope it doesn't fail.

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.642 size:81% position:41%
I hope people buy it.
I hope people read it.

00:02:55.642 --> 00:02:56.442 size:84% position:42% line:92%
I hope people like it."

00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:59.679 align:start size:94% position:6%
And then once that happened,
I'm like, "Oh.

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:00.713 line:92%
Maybe I can write another one.

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:04.617
And, you know, around that time,
I heard this crazy story

00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:09.255 align:start size:94% position:6%
that was true that Sinatra
who had helped get JFK elected

00:03:09.255 --> 00:03:12.859 align:start size:94% position:6%
had wanted President Kennedy
to stay at his compound

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:15.261 align:start size:94% position:6%
in Rancho Mirage,
and he had it all built up

00:03:15.261 --> 00:03:17.463 align:start size:94% position:6%
a helipad
and phone lines and rooms.

00:03:17.463 --> 00:03:20.800
And then Attorney General
Kennedy heard about this

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:23.469 align:start size:94% position:6%
and said, "I'm investigating
organized crime,

00:03:23.469 --> 00:03:26.639 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Sinatra is actually friend
with mobsters.

00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:27.774 line:92%
I don't know about this trip."

00:03:27.774 --> 00:03:30.276 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I heard about this
around that time.

00:03:30.276 --> 00:03:34.581
I'm like, "Well, that's a grea
setting for the second book.

00:03:34.581 --> 00:03:35.682 line:92%
-So, these are, you know,

00:03:35.682 --> 00:03:37.584 align:start size:94% position:6%
a lot of fictional character
in this book,

00:03:37.584 --> 00:03:40.286 align:start size:94% position:6%
but you're also writing abou
real people,

00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:43.223 align:start size:94% position:6%
and yet, in a fictional way,
which I am sure people

00:03:43.223 --> 00:03:45.525 align:start size:94% position:6%
accuse you of doing on CNN
all the time.

00:03:45.525 --> 00:03:47.227 align:start size:94% position:6%
-[ Laughs ]
Yes.

00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:48.995 line:92%
But this is actually fake news

00:03:48.995 --> 00:03:51.631 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Okay, gotcha.
-This is the true fake news.

00:03:51.631 --> 00:03:53.700 align:start size:94% position:6%
All of the stuff that we
reported about President Trump

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:57.303
et cetera, that, believe i
or not, although in many way

00:03:57.303 --> 00:03:59.973 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's stranger than fiction
that actually happened.

00:03:59.973 --> 00:04:04.978
-So, do you enjoy the research
that goes into a book like that,

00:04:04.978 --> 00:04:08.948 align:start size:94% position:6%
and are you also particularl
drawn to that era

00:04:08.948 --> 00:04:11.551 align:start size:94% position:6%
of American politics
and culture?

00:04:11.551 --> 00:04:12.885 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-I love that era.

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:16.856 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's so glamorous
on its face

00:04:16.856 --> 00:04:21.060 size:88% position:44%
but also, underneath it,
really full of menace.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:22.595 line:92%
And I do like the research

00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:27.333
I mean, I got into this business
because I love history.

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:28.334 line:92%
I love it while it's going on.

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:31.204 align:start size:94% position:6%
I love it from 100 years ago
50 years ago.

00:04:31.204 --> 00:04:33.606 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
So it's fun for me.

00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:35.742 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You write about the Rat Pac
in this book.

00:04:35.742 --> 00:04:39.379 line:92%
Who are you in the CNN Rat Pack?

00:04:39.379 --> 00:04:42.148 line:92%
Are you the Sinatra of CNN

00:04:42.148 --> 00:04:43.850 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
Are you the Dean?

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:46.085 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm kind of like
the Joey Bishop.

00:04:46.085 --> 00:04:46.619 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:46.619 --> 00:04:50.156
The funny Jew who goes home to
his wife at the end of the day

00:04:50.156 --> 00:04:53.126 align:start size:94% position:6%
-We thought that too,
but we didn't want to say it

00:04:53.126 --> 00:04:54.193 align:start size:94% position:6%
We were hoping.
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:54.193 --> 00:04:58.298 size:84% position:42%
-We wanted to find out.
-The sweet family man.

00:04:58.298 --> 00:05:00.566 align:start size:94% position:6%
The sweet family man.
-Yeah.

00:05:00.566 --> 00:05:01.367 line:92%
You know, it's interesting

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:04.737
I've heard you say a lot o
the people that were famou

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:07.006 align:start size:94% position:6%
in the time you set this boo
are still very famous.

00:05:07.006 --> 00:05:09.509 size:88% position:44%
It's really interesting.
-Yeah. It's incredible.

00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:12.879 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, so many of the stars
back then,

00:05:12.879 --> 00:05:15.748 align:start size:94% position:6%
and so many of the films
that I write about,

00:05:15.748 --> 00:05:18.518 align:start size:94% position:6%
because when Charlie and
Margaret, my characters, go to

00:05:18.518 --> 00:05:21.688 align:start size:94% position:6%
investigate whether or not
Sinatra is actually mobbed up,

00:05:21.688 --> 00:05:25.391
having been enlisted to do s
by Attorney General Kennedy,

00:05:25.391 --> 00:05:28.328
you know, they go to a movie set
of "The Manchurian Candidate,"

00:05:28.328 --> 00:05:30.596 align:start size:94% position:6%
and they meet people
in addition to the Rat Pack --

00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:33.099 align:start size:94% position:6%
Dean Martin, Peter Lawford
Sammy Davis Jr. --

00:05:33.099 --> 00:05:35.535
they run into -- you know,
there's Alfred Hitchcock.

00:05:35.535 --> 00:05:38.037 size:78% position:39%
There's Tippi Hedren.
There's Janet Leigh.

00:05:38.037 --> 00:05:40.173 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, all of these people.
It's incredible.

00:05:40.173 --> 00:05:43.443 size:88% position:44%
They're still so iconic,
and the movies are still

00:05:43.443 --> 00:05:48.414 size:88% position:44%
a part of our discourse,
part of popular culture.

00:05:48.414 --> 00:05:49.682 size:88% position:44% line:92%
-Well, you're very wise.

00:05:49.682 --> 00:05:52.652 align:start size:94% position:6%
You're very wise to make tha
what your book was about.

00:05:52.652 --> 00:05:53.786 line:92%
I've known you to be a smart guy

00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:56.155 align:start size:94% position:6%
for a long time,
and I am not surprised.

00:05:56.155 --> 00:05:56.989 line:92%
I have a lot more questions.

00:05:56.989 --> 00:05:59.726 align:start size:94% position:6%
We'll be right back
with more from Jake Tapper

